
!Streaming!
    - means the server continuously/progressively sends data to the client over a stream instead of requiring the client to request each next page.

Example

Normal pagination:

Client → Give me 100 records
Server → Records 1–100

Client → Give me next 100
Server → Records 101–200

Client → Give me next 100
Server → Records 201–300

The client repeatedly asks for the next page.

Streaming:

Client → Start stream

Server → Record 1
       → Record 2
       → Record 3
       → Record 4
       → ...

The server progressively sends records through the stream.

Common with gRPC

gRPC supports server streaming, for example:

Client ── GetMessages() ──→ Server

Client ←── Message 1 ────── Server
Client ←── Message 2 ────── Server
Client ←── Message 3 ────── Server
Client ←── Message 4 ────── Server
              ...
Pagination vs Streaming
Pagination	Streaming
Data fetched in pages	Data sent progressively
Client requests next page	Server continues sending
Uses limit/offset/cursor	Uses a stream
Good for search/product lists	Good for real-time or continuous data

Interview definition:

Streaming is a data-delivery approach where the server progressively sends data to the client over a stream rather than requiring repeated requests for separate pages.

One important point: Infinite scrolling is not necessarily streaming. Instagram-like infinite scroll often uses cursor pagination behind the scenes.